module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.libuv

// `close` and `unref` are `Handle` members, and a trait has to be in scope to reach one.
import sh.sysl.libuv.Handle
import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// What a program is told when something outside it asks it to stop.
//
//     onSignal("SIGTERM", () ->
//         print("shutting down")
//         close(server))
//
// **This is how a server is stopped, and nothing else is.** A program with a listening socket in it
// runs until something closes that socket, so the only way it ever ends is from the inside -- and
// every way a deployment has of asking it to stop is a signal. A container stopping, a `systemd`
// unit restarting and Ctrl-C at a terminal are all `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT`, and a program that cannot
// hear one is a program that can only be killed: whatever it was in the middle of answering is
// dropped where it stood.
//
// **It is not a signal handler**, in the sense that word has in C. libuv catches the signal in
// whatever way the platform allows and delivers it to the loop, so what runs here is an ordinary
// slate function at an ordinary time, between one turn of the loop and the next. Nothing about it is
// restricted to what is async-signal-safe: it may print, allocate, close a socket and start a timer,
// which is exactly what a program shutting down needs to do.
//
// **A handler is handed nothing, and the registration is what names the signal.** slate checks the
// count of a call's arguments, so a name passed to the handler would be a parameter every program
// had to write and nearly none would read -- node hands one over because one JavaScript function is
// commonly registered for several signals and JavaScript ignores what it is not expecting. A program
// here that wants one function for two signals registers it twice, and a program that needs to tell
// them apart says so at the two registrations rather than in a branch inside the handler.
//
// ## A watcher does not keep the program alive
//
// **This is node's rule and it is deliberate.** `node -e 'process.on("SIGINT", () => {})'` prints
// nothing and exits at once, and a slate program that registers a handler and does nothing else does
// the same. The alternative is worse than it looks: a script that installs a `SIGTERM` handler out
// of habit would never end, and the reason would be invisible in the source.
//
// So a watcher is `unref`ed and its slot is **not** counted in `outstanding`. What keeps a server
// running is the server -- the listening socket's slot, exactly as before -- and the handler is
// something that happens to be armed while it does.
//
// ## Why this is a table of its own rather than a slot in `event.sysl`'s
//
// `Pending` is what the loop is **waiting for**, and `outstanding` is how many of those there are:
// a slot in it is a reason for the program to still be running. A signal watcher is the opposite
// kind of thing -- something armed for as long as the program happens to last -- so putting one in
// that table would have meant claiming a slot and then undoing the count it exists to keep.
//
// Everything else about the arrangement is the same, and for the same reasons: the collector cannot
// see through an ARC box, so a callback captures a `usize` into this table rather than the slate
// closure it means to call, and the closure itself lives in the slot where the mark phase can reach
// it. A `&libuv.Signal` is a counted box and may not live in the collected heap at all, so it lives
// here too.

// One signal being watched.
private struct Watch
    // Whether this slot is in use. A spent slot is reused rather than grown past.
    live: bool

    // The slate function to call. `Null` once the slot is spent, which is what makes it collectable
    // again -- the flag is what the mark phase reads, and a live-looking slot holding a dead closure
    // would keep it alive forever.
    callback: Value

    // The watcher itself, kept alive here for as long as the program may still be sent the signal.
    watcher: Option[&libuv.Signal]

    // Which occupant of this slot this is, and it is what makes an id safe to hand a program. A
    // spent slot is reused, so an id on its own names a *position* rather than a thing: held across
    // an `offSignal` it would come to mean whatever took the position next, and stopping a handler
    // by a stale id would stop somebody else's. `event.sysl`'s `Pending.gen` is the same field for
    // the same reason.
    gen: usize

// Every signal this program is watching.
//
// **Module storage and a collector root**, for the reason in the header: this table is the only
// thing standing between a registered handler and the sweep.
private var watches: Buf[Watch] = buf()

// The number the next registration will stamp its slot with. Never reused, never reset within a run.
private var watch_generation: usize = 0

// Mark what a signal may still call.
//
// Not `private`: `obj.sysl` roots the collector's walk and this is one of the places it reaches.
mark_watches(h: *gc.Heap)
    for i in 0..<watches.len()
        if watches.at(i).live
            mark_value(h, watches.at(i).callback)

// Give the table back between runs.
//
// **Every watcher is stopped and not merely dropped, and that matters more here than it does for a
// timer.** A handle holds a reference to itself while it is open, so letting go of the last name for
// one stops nothing -- and a `SIGTERM` watcher left armed from a program that has finished would go
// on catching `SIGTERM` for the process it was running in. In the test suite that process is the
// suite, which would then quietly stop being stoppable.
//
// Not `private`: `event.sysl` puts the loop back to nothing between programs and this goes with it.
forget_watches()
    for i in 0..<watches.len()
        stop_watch(i)

    watches.clear()
    watch_generation = 0

// Take a slot, reusing a spent one where there is one. `event.sysl`'s `claim` is the same shape.
private claim_watch(w: Watch) -> usize
    var slot = w

    watch_generation += 1
    slot.gen = watch_generation

    for i in 0..<watches.len()
        if !watches.at(i).live
            watches.set(i, slot)
            return i

    watches.push(slot)
    watches.len() - 1
end claim_watch

// Stop watching, and give the slot back.
//
// **Closing is what stops it.** libuv takes a handle off the signal as it closes, so there is no
// second call to make -- and a handle never closed is one the loop would wait on forever if it were
// counted, which is the rule `event.sysl`'s `surrender` keeps for a timer and a socket.
private stop_watch(id: usize)
    if id >= watches.len() then return

    var w = watches.at(id)

    if !w.live then return

    w.live = false
    w.callback = Null

    w.watcher match
        Some(s) -> s.close(() -> ())
        None -> ()

    w.watcher = None
    watches.set(id, w)
end stop_watch

// Run the slate function in a slot.
//
// **A fault inside a handler stops the program**, as one inside a timer callback does and as an
// uncaught throw does in node. There is no statement to attach it to -- the loop is not running any
// -- so it is reported against the whole run.
private fire_watch(id: usize)
    if id >= watches.len() then return

    val w = watches.at(id)

    if !w.live then return

    val cb = w.callback
    var none: Buf[Value] = buf()
    val mark = root_mark()

    hold(cb)

    apply_callback(cb, none, point(0), "onSignal") match
        Ok(_) -> ()
        Err(s) -> loop_fault = Some(s)

    release(mark)
end fire_watch

// The number a name stands for, which is the platform's and is asked for rather than written down.
//
// **`SIGKILL` and `SIGSTOP` are named here even though neither can be caught**, so that a program
// asking for one is told what is true of it rather than that no such signal exists. A program that
// named `SIGKILL` meant it, and the answer it needs is that nothing of its own will run.
private signal_number(name: string) -> Option[i32]
    if name == "SIGHUP" then return Some(libuv.SIGHUP)
    if name == "SIGINT" then return Some(libuv.SIGINT)
    if name == "SIGQUIT" then return Some(libuv.SIGQUIT)
    if name == "SIGABRT" then return Some(libuv.SIGABRT)
    if name == "SIGKILL" then return Some(libuv.SIGKILL)
    if name == "SIGPIPE" then return Some(libuv.SIGPIPE)
    if name == "SIGALRM" then return Some(libuv.SIGALRM)
    if name == "SIGTERM" then return Some(libuv.SIGTERM)
    if name == "SIGCHLD" then return Some(libuv.SIGCHLD)
    if name == "SIGCONT" then return Some(libuv.SIGCONT)
    if name == "SIGSTOP" then return Some(libuv.SIGSTOP)
    if name == "SIGTSTP" then return Some(libuv.SIGTSTP)
    if name == "SIGUSR1" then return Some(libuv.SIGUSR1)
    if name == "SIGUSR2" then return Some(libuv.SIGUSR2)
    if name == "SIGWINCH" then return Some(libuv.SIGWINCH)

    None
end signal_number

// What a name nobody knows is told about instead. Every catchable one, in the order above.
private val Catchable: string =
    "SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGQUIT, SIGABRT, SIGPIPE, SIGALRM, SIGTERM, SIGCHLD, SIGCONT, SIGTSTP, " +
    "SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2 and SIGWINCH"

// -- what a program calls --------------------------------------------------------------------------

// `onSignal(name, fn)` -- call `fn` every time this program is sent that signal.
//
// The answer is an id, which `offSignal` takes and nothing else reads. That it is a number at all is
// `setTimeout`'s convention, and it carries a generation for that function's reason.
do_on_signal(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    wants(args, 2, at, "onSignal", "a signal name and a function")?

    val name = text_argument(args.at(0), at, "onSignal", "a signal name")?
    val cb = args.at(1)

    cb match
        Fn(_) -> ()
        Native(_) -> ()
        other -> return fault(at, s"`onSignal` needs a function to call, and this is ${other.type_name()}")

    // **Refused by name, before libuv is asked.** `start` on either answers `EINVAL`, which is a
    // sentence about an invalid argument where the truth is about the kernel.
    if name == "SIGKILL" || name == "SIGSTOP"
        return fault(at, s"`${name}` cannot be caught: the kernel acts on it itself, and nothing this program says will run first")

    signal_number(name) match
        None -> fault(at, s"there is no signal called `${name}`; the ones a program may catch are ${Catchable}")
        Some(number) -> watch_signal(number, cb, at)
end do_on_signal

// Arm one watcher, and put the slate function that goes with it in the same slot.
private watch_signal(number: i32, cb: Value, at: Span) -> Step
    libuv.signal() match
        Err(e) -> fault(at, s"this signal could not be watched: ${e}")
        Ok(s) ->
            val id = claim_watch(Watch(true, cb, Some(s), 0))

            // **The closure captures the id and not the value**, which is the arrangement the
            // collector needs: it cannot see through this box, so what the box holds must be
            // something it does not have to see through.
            s.start(number, (n: i32) -> fire_watch(id)) match
                Err(e) ->
                    stop_watch(id)
                    fault(at, s"this signal could not be watched: ${e}")
                Ok(_) ->
                    // **Armed, and not a reason to keep running.** See the header: a program with
                    // nothing but a handler in it ends, exactly as node's does.
                    s.unref()

                    Ok(Int(long(watches.at(id).gen)))
end watch_signal

// `offSignal(id)` -- stop the handler that id names, and go back to whatever the signal did before.
//
// **An id that names nothing is not an error**, which is `clearTimeout`'s rule: a program that stops
// a handler twice, or stops one after the run that registered it, has said something true about what
// it wants and there is nothing to report.
do_off_signal(args: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    wants(args, 1, at, "offSignal", "the id `onSignal` answered")?

    args.at(0) match
        Int(n) ->
            if n >= 0
                for i in 0..<watches.len()
                    if watches.at(i).live && watches.at(i).gen == usize(n)
                        stop_watch(i)
                        break

            Ok(Null)
        other -> fault(at, s"a signal handler is stopped by its id, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_off_signal
